"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[57202],{634821:e=>{e.exports='# Filter Migration\n\nHow Tableau filters map to AI/BI dashboard filters. There are four places filters can land:\n\n1. **Metric view top-level `filter:`** — applied to all queries against the metric view, before measure aggregation.\n2. **Widget-scoped filter** (in the widget\'s query) — applies only to that widget.\n3. **Page-local filter widget** — user-facing control on one page; applies to widgets on that page that share the bound dataset.\n4. **Global filter widget** — placed on the dedicated `pages/global_filters` page; applies across every page in the dashboard for widgets that share the bound dataset(s). Use when the same Tableau filter recurs across multiple dashboards / pages (see "Global vs Page Filter" below).\n\nPick the destination based on the Tableau filter\'s scope and whether the value is meant to be user-controllable.\n\n## Filter Scope Mapping\n\n| Tableau Filter Type        | Destination                                                         | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Data source filter         | Metric view `filter:`                                               | Always-on, dataset-wide, not user-controllable                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| Extract filter             | Metric view `filter:`                                               | Same — baked into the data layer                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| Context filter             | Metric view `filter:` (typically) — ⚠️ semantic gap with FIXED LODs | Affects every dependent sheet; promote to global unless one-sheet-only. **Caveat**: in Tableau a context filter is applied _before_ FIXED LOD grain is computed. Migration pre-computes FIXED LODs as columns in source SQL, so the metric view\'s `filter:` runs _after_ and on already-aggregated values. If a worksheet relies on a context filter narrowing the FIXED grain, results will diverge — call this out in the skipped report. |\n| Worksheet dimension filter | Widget-scoped filter                                                | Sheet-local condition; not a dashboard interaction                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| Worksheet measure filter   | Widget-scoped filter                                                | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Quick filter (interactive) | Dashboard filter widget                                             | User-controllable filter on the page                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Parameter filter           | SKIP                                                                | Parameters are not migratable — see "Parameters" below                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| Relative-date filter       | Dashboard filter widget (date-range)                                | Reproducible via the date-range filter widget                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n\n## Choosing the Filter Widget Type\n\nOnce you\'ve decided a filter belongs on the page (i.e. it\'s user-controllable), pick the widget type from the Tableau filter\'s class and configuration. These match common AI/BI filter widget options.\n\n| Tableau Filter Configuration              | AI/BI Filter Widget Type        |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| Single-value list (radio / dropdown)      | `dropdown` (single-select)      |\n| Multiple-value list (checkboxes)          | `multi-select`                  |\n| Wildcard / search                         | `search` (text input)           |\n| Slider on dimension                       | `slider`                        |\n| Range on quantitative measure / dimension | `range`                         |\n| Range / sliding date                      | `date-range`                    |\n| Relative date (last N days, this month)   | `date-range` with relative mode |\n\nWhen uncertain, default to `multi-select` for categorical dimensions and `date-range` for date dimensions — these cover the most Tableau filter configurations.\n\n## Default Values and "Show All"\n\n- Tableau "All" = no filter applied. AI/BI filter widgets express this as no default selection. Don\'t pre-select every value.\n- Tableau-set defaults (specific values selected on dashboard load) → set the filter widget\'s default value(s) accordingly.\n- "Show All Values in Database" vs "Show Only Relevant Values" — AI/BI filter widgets show all values by default. There is no direct "relevant values" mode; document if the Tableau dashboard relied on it.\n\n## Global vs Page Filter\n\nA dashboard filter widget can be placed on a single page (page-local) or hoisted to a dedicated global page that every other page shares (effectively dashboard-wide). Choose by counting where the filter appears in the source workbook:\n\n- **Filter appears in only one Tableau dashboard / on one target page** → page-local filter widget on that page.\n- **Filter appears across multiple Tableau dashboards or shows up on > ~50% of target pages** → hoist to a global filter widget. Either:\n  - Add a dedicated "Global Filters" page at the start of the dashboard whose only contents are the shared filter widgets, or\n  - Restate the same filter widget on each page that uses it (acceptable when only 2–3 pages share it, and avoids the extra page).\n\nPhase B step 6 — Enumerate filters — produces this per-filter usage count. Decide before dispatching per-page calls so each page\'s prompt contains only the filters that page actually owns.\n\n## Cross-Filter Behavior\n\nTableau "Apply to Worksheets" controls which sheets a quick filter affects:\n\n| Tableau "Apply To"         | AI/BI Equivalent                                                             |\n| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| All Using This Data Source | Filter applies to every widget that uses the same dataset (default behavior) |\n| Selected Worksheets        | Wire the filter to specific widgets only                                     |\n| Only This Worksheet        | Convert to a widget-scoped filter instead of a page filter                   |\n\nWhen you delegate to the dashboard sub-agent (see `dashboard_build.md`), state the filter\'s wiring explicitly: which widgets it applies to. The sub-agent does not infer this from the metric view.\n\n## Parameters\n\nTableau parameters used as filters (`[Parameters].[Date Choice]`) cannot be migrated. Skip them with documentation:\n\n```yaml\ncomment: |\n  Skipped filters:\n  - SKIPPED: Date Choice filter; Reason: Parameter-driven filter not supported; Original: [Parameters].[Date Choice]\n```\n\nIf a parameter purely drives a calculated field (not a filter), skip the calculation per the "Skip Criteria" decision tree.\n\n## Filter-on-Filter\n\nTableau supports cascading filters where one filter\'s options depend on another\'s selection. AI/BI filter widgets do not natively cascade — selecting a region does not narrow a city dropdown to that region\'s cities.\n\nApproach:\n\n1. Migrate each filter independently.\n2. Document the loss in the migration report\'s "Skipped" section if the cascade is meaningful (e.g. region → city).\n3. If the cascade is critical, suggest the user use a region prefix in the city values (e.g. "West / San Francisco") so multi-select still narrows visually.\n\n## Action Filters\n\nTableau action filters (clicking a mark on one chart filters another) have no direct AI/BI equivalent. Document as skipped.\n\n## Building the Filter into the Output\n\n**For metric view destinations** (`filter:` field):\n\n```yaml\nfilter: client_processor = \'fis\' AND deconversion_date IS NULL\n```\n\nCombine multiple Tableau filters of the same scope into a single `AND`-joined expression.\n\n**For widget-scoped or page filters**: pass the filter into the per-page sub-agent prompt under that widget\'s `filters:` block (widget-scoped) or in the page-level filter list (page filter widget). See `dashboard_build.md` for the prompt format.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- **Promoting too aggressively to the metric view filter**: only the data-source / extract / global-context filters belong there. A worksheet dimension filter is local to that widget.\n- **Over-pre-selecting defaults**: if Tableau showed "All", leave the AI/BI widget with no default selection.\n- **Forgetting to wire page filters to widgets**: a filter widget on the page does nothing unless you tell the sub-agent which widgets it filters. Be explicit.\n'}}]);
